Agent Response: Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s likely deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also note where I need clarification to give exact numbers.

Short summary (likely outcome)
- You’ll almost certainly take the Head‑of‑Household standard deduction for 2023 ($20,800) unless you have many other itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, large property taxes, big state tax payments, or large medical expenses) beyond the $800 charity you listed.
- Your home office (165 sq ft exclusive) can be deducted because you’re self‑employed (IT contracting). That deduction will reduce your Schedule C net income (and therefore income tax and self‑employment tax). You can use either the simplified method (165 sq ft × $5 = $825) or the actual method (about 21.15% of rent/utilities/etc. — roughly $4,300 of rent alone), whichever produces the larger allowable deduction once you include other eligible home expenses.
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500, but it phases out for MAGI between $75k and $90k (single/HOH). With your W‑2 + contracting income + interest, you’re likely near or above the phaseout threshold so you may not be able to claim the student interest deduction — but this depends on precise numbers and what adjustments (half SE tax, business expenses, home office) reduce your MAGI.
- If the $950 is business expenses for your contracting work, you should deduct it on Schedule C; if it’s unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, those are not deductible under current law.
- Expect to use: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (business income/expenses), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and either Form 8829 (if using actual home‑office method) or the simplified method reported on Schedule C. Also consider Form 8995 (qualified business income deduction) if you have QBI. State return: Arizona Form 140.

What I used / quick estimate examples
(rough, to illustrate effects — I can compute exact with confirmed numbers)
- W‑2 wages: $84,900
- Self‑employment income (gross): $12,500
- Interest income: $1,450 + $480 = $1,930
Scenario A — simplified home office + $950 business expenses:
- Home office simplified: $825
- Other business expenses: $950
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 12,500 − 1,775 = 10,725
- Rough SE tax ≈ $1,516 → half SE tax deduction ≈ $758
- AGI before student loan interest ≈ 84,900 + 10,725 + 1,930 − 758 ≈ $96,797 → above the $90k cutoff, so student loan interest deduction likely phased out.
Scenario B — actual home office (rent share only ~21.15%):
- Rent per year 1,720×12 = $20,640 × 0.2115 ≈ $4,366 + $950 expenses = $5,316
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 12,500 − 5,316 = 7,184
- SE tax ≈ $1,016 → half SE tax ≈ $508
- AGI before student loan interest ≈ 84,900 + 7,184 + 1,930 − 508 ≈ $93,506 → still above $90k in this rough calc
Conclusion: in both quick scenarios you’d probably be over the student interest phaseout, but the actual method could reduce your taxable income more if you have other shared home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance portion, internet) to apportion to the office. If your total itemized deductions (charity + SALT etc.) exceed $20,800 then itemize, otherwise take the standard deduction.

Specific guidance and rules
- Home office: allowed for self‑employed contractors if used regularly and exclusively for business. Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5 × square footage (165 sq ft) = $825. No Form 8829 required; enter on Schedule C.
  - Actual method: prorate a percentage of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Use Form 8829 to compute. The actual-method deduction can be larger but requires more documentation.
- Student loan interest: max $2,500 above‑the‑line (Schedule 1). Phases out for MAGI $75k–$90k (single/HOH) — above $90k you can’t claim it. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid in 2023; older years’ interest doesn't matter for this year.
- Charitable: $800 is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. For most people in your situation that’s less than the HOH standard deduction, so it will not help unless you have other itemizable amounts that push you above $20,800.
- Unreimbursed expenses: not deductible for employees under current law. If the $950 are business expenses for your contracting work, report them on Schedule C.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of SE tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI): you may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from your contracting work. This is reported on Form 8995 (simple) or 8995‑A. Because your total taxable income appears below the phase‑in thresholds, you may be able to claim the full 20% of QBI from your net Schedule C income (after allowable deductions).
- State filing: file Arizona Form 140 (AZ state taxes may be deductible as SALT up to $10k on federal Schedule A if you itemize).

Required forms (typical)
- Federal: Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (adjustments: student loan interest, half SE tax if applicable)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report contracting income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (if using actual method for home office)
  - Form 8995 (if claiming QBI deduction)
  - Schedule A (only if itemizing)
- Documents you should have: W‑2 from TechStart; 1099‑NEC or records of 1099s from your contracting clients (or bank deposits if no 1099); 1099‑INTs from Goldman Sachs Marcus and E*TRADE; Form 1098‑E from Navient if you paid interest in 2023; receipts for charitable donations; documentation of rent/utility payments and home‑office usage; receipts for the $950 business expenses.
- State: Arizona Form 140.

What I need from you to give a precise recommendation and run the actual math
- Were the $4,400 student loan interest payments made in 2023? Do you have a 1098‑E for 2023 from Navient showing interest paid in 2023? (If the $4,400 is a cumulative amount from 2016–2020, it is not relevant.)
- Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses for your contracting business (i.e., business expenses you paid) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job? If they’re business expenses, provide a short list of what they are.
- Do you have any other itemizable expenses for 2023 (mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax payments, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI)?
- Did you receive 1099‑NEC forms from either client? Did you make any estimated tax payments for 2023?
- Do you want me to compute an exact tax estimate (federal and Arizona) using the precise numbers and either home office method? I can run the numbers if you confirm the items above.

Other recommendations
- Keep solid documentation for the home office (floor plan, lease, utility bills, notes about exclusive use).
- If you didn’t make estimated tax payments during 2023 for your contracting income, you may owe taxes and possibly a penalty; we can estimate that.
- If you want, I can compute two scenarios (simplified vs. actual home office) and show whether you keep the standard deduction or itemize and estimate total federal tax owed/refund.

Would you like me to:
1) Run exact tax estimates (I’ll need answers to the clarification questions above), or
2) Draft a short email you can send to Navient requesting a 1098‑E, or
3) Draft an organized checklist of documents to gather for your tax preparer?

Tell me which and I’ll proceed.
