Thanks — I can walk through this. I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical summary of how your 2023 items look, the likely best choices, and the forms you’ll need. I’ll also ask a couple of quick clarifying questions at the end.

Quick facts you gave (used below)
- Filing status: Head of household (AZ, Phoenix)  
- W-2 wages (TechStart): $84,900  
- Self‑employment / contracting income: $12,500 (2 clients)  
- Interest: Goldman Sachs Marcus $1,450 + E*TRADE $480 = $1,930  
- Home: rent $1,720/month; total home = 780 sq ft; exclusive home‑office = 165 sq ft  
- Deductions/other: $4,400 student loan interest (Navient, 2016–2020), $800 charitable (receipt), $950 unreimbursed expenses

1) Standard vs itemize
- 2023 standard deduction (Head of Household) = $20,800.
- Your known itemizable amounts (charity $800) are far below the standard deduction. Unless you have substantial other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses, etc.), you should take the standard deduction.
- Therefore: take the standard deduction in almost all likely scenarios.

2) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (adjustment to income) and phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 for single/HOH taxpayers. 
- Your income picture (wages + contracting + interest) puts you above the $90,000 phaseout threshold, so you likely cannot claim any student‑loan interest deduction for 2023.
- Clarify: the student‑loan deduction applies to interest paid during 2023. When you wrote $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2020), do you mean you actually paid $4,400 in interest in 2023, or is that an aggregate from prior years? If you paid interest in 2023 but your MAGI is still above $90k, it’s likely phased out.

3) Home‑office deduction (self-employed contractor)
You can use the home‑office deduction only for the portion of your home used regularly and exclusively for business. You can choose:
- Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 165 sq ft × $5 = $825.
- Actual method: business percentage × actual home expenses (rent, utilities, etc.). Your business % = 165 / 780 = 21.15%.

Using only rent (you gave rent; if you have utilities/property insurance you can include their business portion too):
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Business portion of rent = 21.1538% × $20,640 ≈ $4,369.

Comparing simplified vs actual (using only rent + your $950 business expenses):
- Actual method:
  - Home office portion of rent ≈ $4,369
  - Other self‑employment expenses = $950
  - Total SE expenses ≈ $5,319 → Net self‑employment income ≈ $12,500 − $5,319 = $7,181
  - Estimated self‑employment tax ≈ $1,016; half is deductible (~$508)
  - Rough AGI (after half SE tax) ≈ $93,503 → taxable after standard deduction ≈ $72,703
- Simplified method:
  - Home office deduction = $825
  - Other SE expenses = $950 → total = $1,775 → Net SE income ≈ $10,725
  - SE tax ≈ $1,515; half deductible (~$758)
  - Rough AGI ≈ $96,797 → taxable after standard deduction ≈ $75,997

Conclusion: based on the rent numbers you provided, the actual method (allocating rent) yields a larger deduction and lowers taxable income by several thousand dollars versus the simplified method. If you have additional home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs), the actual method will become even more favorable. Use actual method if you can substantiate the allocation and have records/receipts.

Note: the home‑office deduction is claimed on Schedule C. If you use the actual method you typically complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C; for the simplified method you report the simplified amount directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

4) Treatment of the $950 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If those $950 are business expenses related to your contracting (supplies, software, travel for clients, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your net self‑employment income (we included them above).
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W‑2 job, those are not deductible on the federal return for 2018–2025 (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions), so they would not be deductible.
- Please confirm whether the $950 is for contracting or for W‑2 employment.

5) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $12,500 contracting income and business expenses (including the home office deduction if you use simplified or direct entry).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax and the deductible half of SE tax.
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual method for business use of home (calculates allowable home‑office deduction). If you use simplified, you typically do not use Form 8829.
- Form 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC: you should have received 1099‑NEC from each client if they paid you $600+; include the income even if you didn’t get 1099s.
- Form 1099‑INT: banks (Marcus, E*TRADE) will issue these — include interest income.
- State: Arizona individual return (AZ Form 140 or the current AZ return form) — AZ starts from federal AGI with state adjustments.

6) Self‑employment tax and estimated payments
- Net self‑employment income will be subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE income). We estimated SE tax between about $1,015 and $1,515 depending on method.
- If you expect the contracting income to continue or your tax withholding won’t cover your total tax liability, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties or increase W‑4 withholding.

7) Records to keep
- Rent receipts/leases and records showing square footage and exclusive use.
- Receipts for the $950 business expenses (invoices, credit card statements).
- Bank 1099‑INTs and any 1099‑NEC forms you received.
- Charitable receipt(s) for the $800 (keep even if you end up taking the standard deduction).
- Student loan interest Form 1098‑E from Navient for 2023, if available (shows interest paid in 2023).

8) Next steps / recommendations
- Confirm two items for me:
  1) Was the $4,400 of student loan interest paid in 2023, or is that an aggregate from earlier years? (Only 2023 interest payments matter for your 2023 return.)
  2) Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses associated with your contracting work (i.e., business expenses) or with your W‑2 employment?
- If you’d like, I can:
  - Run a precise federal tax estimate for 2023 using either home office method and show the tax difference, or
  - Draft an email summary to your tax preparer with the numbers and supporting questions, or
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to upload to your tax pro.

Privacy/compliance note
- This message includes W‑2 and 1099‑type income details. Our system is configured to forward such information for compliance review. If you’d prefer I do not forward this, tell me now. Otherwise I’ll proceed per the configured process.

Would you like me to (A) run a full estimated tax calculation now, (B) draft an email to your tax preparer summarizing everything, or (C) just answer any clarifying questions first?

