Thanks — that’s enough to give a clear, practical plan and a rough calculation. I’ll summarize what you can (probably) deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, which forms you’ll need, and a few follow-up questions so I can give exact numbers.

Quick high-level conclusions
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800) rather than itemizing, because your deductible items shown (charitable $800, maybe state tax or mortgage interest not listed) probably total less than $20,800.
- Your IT contracting income (the $12,500) is reported on Schedule C (self-employment). That means you can take a home office deduction (if the space is used exclusively for the business), and you’ll owe self-employment tax on net SE income (Schedule SE). You may also be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction on that net business income.
- The student-loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) phases out for single/head-of-household filers starting at MAGI $75,000 and is eliminated at $90,000 (2023 rules). With the income amounts you gave, your MAGI is likely above $90k so you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction unless additional above-the-line adjustments or business expense deductions lower your MAGI below the threshold. We can test scenarios if you want.
- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” — whether they are business expenses for the contracting work or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job — matters. If they are business expenses for the contracting work, they reduce Schedule C net profit. If they are W-2 unreimbursed employee expenses they are not deductible on your 2023 federal return (suspended for most taxpayers since 2018).

How to treat the home office
You have an exclusive home office of 165 sq ft in a 780 sq ft home.
- Two options: simplified method or actual expenses.
  - Simplified: $5 x 165 sq ft = $825 deduction (no Form 8829 required).
  - Actual-expense method: take the business’s percentage of allowable home expenses. Your business percentage = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%. If you rent at $1,720/month → annual rent = $20,640 and the rent portion attributable to the office ≈ $20,640 × 21.15% ≈ $4,366. You could also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. to the business using the same percentage. (If you owned the house, mortgage interest, property taxes, depreciation, and other items would be handled; depreciation in particular requires Form 8829.)
- Practical rule: compare the simplified $825 to the actual-expense total. Use the actual method if it gives a larger deduction (it usually will if your rent is substantial and you have utilities/other allocable costs). If you claim actual expenses you’ll use Form 8829 to report the breakdown and carry the result to Schedule C.

What you should report for the contracting activity
- Report gross 1099/self-employment income $12,500 on Schedule C.
- Subtract deductible business expenses (home office deduction, actual business expenses such as supplies, software, mileage, phone portion, contractor insurance, etc.). If the $950 is a legitimate business expense, include it here.
- Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 as business income and is used to compute self-employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Self-employment tax is 15.3% on net SE earnings (with the usual adjustments); half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.
- QBI deduction: you may be able to take a 20% deduction of qualified business income (subject to rules). At your income level this is likely available, which further reduces taxable income (not AGI).

Student loan interest
- You listed $4,400 paid to Navient (2016–2020). Only interest actually paid in the 2023 tax year counts. Also, the deduction maximum is $2,500 (and phase-outs apply).
- Given the incomes you listed, your MAGI is likely above the $90,000 cutoff for head-of-household in 2023, which would eliminate eligibility. We can calculate exact MAGI after accounting for the allowable self-employment deductions and half SE tax to see whether any partial deduction applies.

Charitable contribution
- The $800 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for HOH is $20,800, you would generally not itemize for only $800 of charitable donations (unless you have other sizable deductible items such as state/local tax, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses).

Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (individual tax return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — student loan interest deduction, and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $12,500 contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — calculates SE tax and half-deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely based on the amounts you gave)

Rough numerical example (illustrative, uses assumptions)
I’ll show two quick estimates depending on whether the $950 are business expenses or nondeductible employee expenses, and using the actual home-office rent allocation:

Assumptions:
- W-2 wages: $84,900
- SE income: $12,500
- Interest income (Marcus + E*TRADE): $1,450 + $480 = $1,930
- Home-office (actual from rent only): ≈ $4,366
- Scenario A: $950 is a business expense; Scenario B: $950 is NOT deductible

Scenario A (950 is business expense)
- Business deductions = home office 4,366 + other business 950 = 5,316
- Schedule C net profit ≈ 12,500 − 5,316 = 7,184
- Self-employment tax (approx) ≈ $1,016 → half deductible ≈ $508
- Total income before SE adjustment ≈ 84,900 + 7,184 + 1,930 = 93,? (≈94,014)
- AGI after half SE tax ≈ 94,014 − 508 = ≈93,506
- Standard deduction HOH = 20,800 → taxable income ≈ 72,706
- QBI (20% of net profit) ≈ $1,437 — this reduces taxable income further (subject to rules)

Scenario B (950 is unreimbursed employee expense — nondeductible)
- Business deductions = home office 4,366 only
- Net profit ≈ 12,500 − 4,366 = 8,134
- SE tax and half deduction will be slightly larger; AGI still likely > $90k
- Student loan interest deduction still likely disallowed

Bottom line from the example: you’re probably better off taking the standard deduction, using Schedule C and either the actual or simplified home-office method (choose whichever gives the larger deduction), and reporting SE tax/QBI. The student loan interest deduction is likely not available with the raw numbers you provided, but small changes (e.g., additional business expenses, IRA contributions, or retirement plan contributions) could reduce MAGI and change that.

Next steps / what I need from you to finalize exact numbers
1. Are the $950 unreimbursed expenses related to your IT contracting (business expenses) or to your W-2 job (nondeductible employee expenses)?
2. Did you pay any other business expenses in 2023 (software, subscriptions, travel, mileage, phone percentage, equipment, contractor insurance)? If yes, list totals.
3. For home: you said you pay rent $1,720/month — confirm you rent (not own).
4. How much actual student loan interest did you pay in 2023 specifically (not earlier years)? The $4,400 amount seems to span multiple years.
5. Do you want me to run precise calculations with these numbers and produce a tax-preparation checklist (and an item-by-item fill-in form you could give to a preparer)?

